ues (nasty remarks, wrong tone) affect crew cooperation and professional communication. Adaptability unknown.\n\nCandidate A: technical competence (anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, spatial vision, leadership). Reliability unknown. Stress resilience unknown. Communication? Not open to new ideas, maybe poor adaptability. No mention of cooperation.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility (takes seriously), problem solving, concentration, responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience). Communication issues: hot temper, loner (crew cooperation). Technical competence unknown.\n\nThus Candidate C seems strongest overall, but communication concerns are serious for crew cooperation. Candidate B also has communication issues. Candidate A has unknown reliability and stress. Candidate D also has crew cooperation issues.\n\nThus likely we stay with Candidate C, but we could ask for more info on communication or teamwork. We could ask another panel member about Candidate C's crew cooperation or adaptability. But we can also raise concerns about communication and ask for more evidence.\n\nWe need to produce a public message: maybe we say we agree with Candidate C but note concerns about communication and ask for input from others. Vote for Candidate C.\n\nThus:\n\nPUBLIC_MESSAGE: I agree with Agent 3's provisional lead on Candidate C. The evidence of 100% reliability, calmness under pressure, quick correct decisions, plus technical competence (understands complicated technology) and excellent attention skills, align with core criteria. While communication difficulty and egocentric attitude are concerns, they are secondary to safety-critical strengths. I would still vote for Candidate C, but we should probe further on crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nMETADATA_JSON: vote Candidate C.\n\nLet's produce that."}
